#: ../../odin.rst:1
msgid "Documenting Odin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:4
msgid "The Odin domain gives packages, procedures, types, and members named targets. Guidedog also generates API pages by parsing Odin source. It does not run the compiler or execute the package. A generated signature still needs a useful contract."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:8
msgid "Describing objects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:11
msgid "A package is the context of what follows it, as a Python module is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:33
msgid "Signatures are written as Odin writes declarations, on one line, and shown the same way. This is how the ones below look:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:48
msgid "Measures a square."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:50
msgid "Its side."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:51
msgid "Its area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:52
msgid "Whether its kind is known."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:54
msgid "The directives, and the signatures they take:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:56
msgid "``odin:package`` and ``odin:currentpackage``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:57
msgid "``core:fmt``, or a path below a project's roots, such as ``readers/sphinx``. The package's import path names its objects: ``core:fmt.println``. ``odin:currentpackage`` changes the context without a target. ``None`` clears it. Options: ``:synopsis:``, ``:platform:``, ``:deprecated:``, ``:no-index:``, ``:imports:``, and ``:private:``. ``:imports:`` lists the import aliases the package's signatures use, as ``alias=package`` pairs (``gd=core rst=readers/rst``), so ``gd.Node_Id`` links to ``core.Node_Id``. ``:private:`` lists the package's private types and the private members of its procedure groups (``State Rules catalog_text``). Its signatures show them as text, since they have no descriptions to link to. The generated pages list them automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:68
msgid "``odin:procedure`` (or ``odin:proc``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:69
msgid "``name :: proc(params) -> results``, with attributes before the name (``@(require_results)``), ``#force_inline`` before ``proc``, a calling convention (``proc \"c\" (...)``), parameters with defaults (``x := 1``, ``x: int = 1``), ``$T`` polymorphic parameters, ``..`` variadics, ``using``, ``#c_vararg``, named results, tags (``#optional_ok``), and a ``where`` clause. ``name(params) -> results`` is short for the same."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:76
msgid "``odin:procgroup``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:77
msgid "``name :: proc{a, b}``: each member links to its procedure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:79
msgid "``odin:struct``, ``odin:union``, ``odin:enum``, ``odin:bitset``, ``odin:bitfield``, ``odin:type``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:80
msgid "``Name :: struct($T: typeid) #packed``, ``Name :: union #no_nil {A, B}``, ``Name :: enum u8``, ``Name :: bit_set[Flag; u8]``, ``Name :: bit_field u32``, and for ``odin:type``, distinct types and aliases (``Handle :: distinct uintptr``, ``Callback :: proc(x: int) -> bool``). A type's content holds its members."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:85
msgid "``odin:field`` and ``odin:enumerator``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:86
msgid "Inside a type: ``name: Type`` with a tag (``name: string `json:\"n\"```) or a bit size (``low: u8 | 3``), and ``Name`` or ``Name = 3``. Their names are ``Type.member``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:90
msgid "``odin:const``, ``odin:var``, ``odin:foreign``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:91
msgid "``NAME :: 64`` or ``NAME : int : 64``; ``name: Type``, ``name := value``, or ``name: Type = value``; ``libc \"system:c\"`` for a foreign import."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:94
msgid "Every object takes the options objects take in Sphinx's domains: ``:no-index:``, ``:no-index-entry:``, ``:no-contents-entry:``, and ``:no-typesetting:``. It also takes these of its own: ``:package:`` (describe it in another package), ``:private:`` and ``:deprecated: message`` (shown as ``@(private)`` and ``@(deprecated=\"message\")`` unless the signature has them), ``:availability: Windows, Linux`` (a first line of the content saying where it exists), and ``:foreign: libc`` (a procedure of a foreign block). A directive with several signature lines describes one object whose signature differs between targets. Only the first line gets the id and the index entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:103
msgid "Names in type positions link to the types they name. They are looked up first in the package and type the signature is written in, then as written. Built-in types (``int``, ``string``, ``rawptr``, ``typeid``, ``any``, ...), keywords, polymorphic names (``$T``, and ``T`` after it, in the signature or in the type the object is nested in), array lengths, default values, constants, tags, and ``where`` clauses are shown as written, never as links."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:110
msgid "Roles and ids"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:113
msgid "``:odin:pkg:``, ``:odin:proc:``, ``:odin:type:`` (structs, unions, enums, bit sets, bit fields, and other types), ``:odin:const:``, ``:odin:var:``, ``:odin:field:``, ``:odin:enumerator:``, and ``:odin:obj:`` (any of them) link to objects. As in the Python domain, ``~`` shows only the last part (``:odin:proc:`~core:fmt.println``` shows ``println``), and a leading ``.`` finds the name at the end of any object's name. A name is looked up in the reference's type and package first, then as written, then after a package path's ``:`` or ``/``. So ``fmt.println`` finds ``core:fmt.println``, and ``sphinx.Config`` finds ``readers/sphinx.Config``, as Odin code names a package by its last segment. An import alias the current package declares with ``:imports:`` is followed first. With ``default-domain:: odin`` (or ``primary_domain = \"odin\"``), the roles need no ``odin:``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:125
msgid "An object's id is Sphinx's ``make_id`` of ``odin-`` and its full name: ``odin-core-fmt.println``, ``odin-readers-sphinx.Config.docname``. Case and dots are kept, as Sphinx keeps them for Python ids. The ``:`` and ``/`` of the import path become hyphens. Ids are therefore readable in URLs, the same in every build, and distinct for two packages with the same last name. A package's id is ``odin-package-`` and its path. Index entries read \"println (procedure in core:fmt)\". ``objects.inv`` lists every object with the domain ``odin`` and its type (``odin:procedure``, ``odin:struct``, ``odin:field``, ...), packages first, as Sphinx lists modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:134
msgid "Info fields in the content are grouped as in the other domains: ``:param name:`` (with ``:type name:``), ``:result name:`` for a named result, ``:returns:``, and ``:rtype:``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:137
msgid "Generating from source"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:140
msgid "We tell Guidedog where our packages are, relative to the source folder:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:147
msgid "A package below ``odin_autoapi_dirs`` is named by its path there (``src/shapes/round`` is ``shapes/round``). A package in a collection is named as Odin imports it (``core:strings``). The generating directives then work as autodoc's do:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:161
msgid "``odin:autopackage`` writes the package, its doc comment, and with ``:members:`` its declarations (all of them, or those listed). ``odin:autoproc``, ``odin:autoprocgroup``, ``odin:autotype``, ``odin:autoconst``, and ``odin:autovar`` write one declaration, named in the current package or as ``package.name``. Options: ``:members:``, ``:undoc-members:``, ``:private-members:`` (``@(private)`` declarations, which are left out otherwise), ``:exclude-members:``, ``:member-order:``, and ``:no-index:``. ``:member-order:`` is ``source`` (the default, by file name and then position), ``alphabetical``, or ``groupwise``. ``groupwise`` heads the groups \"Types\", \"Procedures\", \"Procedure groups\", \"Constants\", \"Variables\", and \"Foreign imports\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:172
msgid "Doc comments are the ``//`` lines directly above a declaration, or a ``/* */`` block there, and a field's or enumerator's comment at the end of its line. They are plain text, and become reStructuredText as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:176
msgid "Paragraphs stay paragraphs. A list of ``- item`` lines stays a list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:177
msgid "An indented block (a tab or spaces) after a blank line, or after a line ending in a colon (``Example:``), is a ``code-block:: odin``, or ``text`` after ``Output:``. A line indented more than the text directly after a paragraph line continues it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:180
msgid "``Inputs:`` followed by ``- name: text`` items become ``:param name:`` fields, and ``Returns:`` items ``:result name:`` fields (or ``:returns:`` when they have no names), as Odin's core library writes them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:183
msgid "A line starting ``NOTE:`` or ``WARNING:`` is a note or a warning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:184
msgid "```code``` is literal text. Every other character reStructuredText would read as markup (``*``, ``|``, a ``_`` ending a word, ...) is escaped, so the comment reads as written and never warns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:188
msgid "API pages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:191
msgid "With ``odin_autoapi_dirs`` set, every package below those folders also gets a page of its own. The build generates it as it reads it, like sphinx-autoapi does. ``api/shapes`` holds ``odin:autopackage:: shapes`` with ``odin_autoapi_options``. ``api/index`` lists every package with its synopsis. The pages are documents of the project (in the toctree, search, the general index, ``singlehtml``, and the PDF book). They are never written into the source folder. Settings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:198
msgid "``odin_autoapi_dirs``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:199
msgid "The folders whose packages get pages, relative to the source folder. Only files below them (and below ``odin_collections``) are read, and a link leading elsewhere is refused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:203
msgid "``odin_collections``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:204
msgid "A table of collection names and folders, for directive arguments such as ``core:strings``. These packages get no pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:207
msgid "``odin_autoapi_root``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:208
msgid "The folder of the pages, ``\"api\"`` by default. A document of the source folder with a page's name keeps its name, with a warning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:211
msgid "``odin_autoapi_options``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:212
msgid "``[\"members\", \"undoc-members\"]`` by default. ``private-members`` may be added."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:214
msgid "``odin_autoapi_member_order``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:215
msgid "``\"source\"`` (default), ``\"alphabetical\"``, or ``\"groupwise\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:217
msgid "``odin_autoapi_add_toctree_entry``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:218
msgid "``true`` (default): ``api/index`` joins the root document's first toctree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:220
msgid "``odin_autoapi_generate_api_docs``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:221
msgid "``true`` (default). ``false`` reads the folders for the directives only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:223
msgid "Types from packages we do not document (``core:io.Writer``) cannot be linked. With ``-n``, we silence them with ``nitpick_ignore_regex = [[\"odin:type\", \"(core|base):.*\"]]``. Guidedog's own API reference, ``docs/api``, is now built the same way from Rust source with ``rust_autoapi_crates``. See :doc:`rust`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:228
msgid "Platforms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:231
msgid "A package is documented once for every target Odin builds, as Odin itself sees its files. A file named ``*_windows.odin``, ``*_linux_arm64.odin``, or ``*_amd64.odin`` builds only for those targets. ``#+build linux, darwin`` and ``#+build !windows`` tags (and the older ``//+build``) narrow it. A file-scope ``when ODIN_OS == .Windows`` block counts for its targets (and its ``else`` for the others) when its condition only compares ``ODIN_OS`` and ``ODIN_ARCH``. Files named ``*_test.odin``, and files tagged ``#+ignore``, are left out. A declaration made for every target the package builds for is shown once. One made for some targets says which, \"Availability: Windows\". One whose signature differs between targets is shown once per signature, each with its targets, the first with the id. A package that builds only for some targets says so with its ``:platform:``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:243
msgid "Rebuilding"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:246
msgid "Every Odin file a page read is a dependency of that page. When we edit a package's source, the next build reads again only the pages that document it. The package index is read again when a package appears, disappears, or changes its synopsis. ``guidedog serve`` checks modification times in ``odin_autoapi_dirs`` and ``odin_collections`` before serving an HTML page. We refresh the browser after editing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:252
msgid "Limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:255
msgid "The source is parsed, never compiled or type-checked. Types are shown as written, a constant's value as written (and left out beyond 100 characters), and nothing is inferred. Whether ``A :: B`` declares a type or a constant is told from the names: a capitalized name not in capitals throughout, or a built-in type, is a type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:259
msgid "Conditions of ``when`` blocks other than comparisons of ``ODIN_OS`` and ``ODIN_ARCH`` are not evaluated: both branches are documented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:261
msgid "A declaration that mentions a private type shows it as text, since private declarations have no entries unless ``:private-members:`` is given. The generated package lists them in ``:private:``, so ``-n`` has nothing to report."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:264
msgid "Guidedog reads declarations with its own port of Odin's tokenizer and parser. The parser skips procedure bodies. A syntax error in a declaration is reported (``odin.autodoc.syntax``), and what could be read is still documented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:267
msgid "A declaration nested deeper than 160 levels of brackets or types is far deeper than real code. Its file is left out with the warning ``odin.autodoc.nesting``, and the rest of its package is documented. Long flat chains (``a + b + ...``) and anything inside procedure bodies do not count. A ``when`` condition is evaluated to 64 levels of ``&&``, ``||`` and ``!``. A longer one counts as not evaluated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../odin.rst:272
msgid "A file larger than the per-file limit is left out with ``odin.autodoc.memory``."
msgstr ""
